---
description: Follow the unslop skill on user-facing writing, and label each technical claim
alwaysApply: true
---

Before a reply to the user, a pull request description, or a commit message, read `.claude/skills/unslop/SKILL.md` and rewrite the draft to follow it.

The skill file sets `disable-model-invocation: true`. Cursor leaves it out of the automatic skill list. This rule is what applies the skill when nobody typed `/unslop`.

When a technical report mentions a behavior, name one of these four states in the same sentence.

Observation. A fact about the current code. You are not claiming it is broken, and you are not claiming you changed it.

Unresolved. The current code does the wrong thing. Name the file and the command or function that does it. Say that the fix is not in a commit yet.

Proposed. A change you recommend. Name the files it would touch. Say that you have not made the change.

Done. A change that is in a commit. Name the check you ran and what it printed. A test that records today's behavior is done only for that recording. It is not a fix for a mismatch the test still allows.

Do not use a planning word such as "contract", "fixture", or "sequence" until the sentence has also given the file path and a concrete command.
